<article class="env-example" id="0ENT">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0ENT">Example <span data-tag="0ENT">72.6.10</span>.</a> This is a continuation of Morphisms of Spaces, Example <a href="/tag/05Z6" data-tag="05Z6">67.53.3</a>. Consider the algebraic space $X = \mathbf{A}^1_ k/\{ t \sim -t \mid t \not= 0\} $. This is a smooth algebraic space over the field $k$. There is a universal homeomorphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  X \longrightarrow \mathbf{A}^1_ k = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k[t])  \]
</div>
  <p> which is an isomorphism over $\mathbf{A}^1_ k \setminus \{ 0\} $. We conclude that $X$ is Noetherian and integral. Since $\dim (X) = 1$, we see that the prime divisors of $X$ are the closed points of $X$. Consider the unique closed point $x \in |X|$ lying over $0 \in \mathbf{A}^1_ k$. Since $X \setminus \{ x\} $ maps isomorphically to $\mathbf{A}^1 \setminus \{ 0\} $ we see that the classes in $\text{Cl}(X)$ of closed points different from $x$ are zero. However, the divisor of $t$ on $X$ is $2[x]$. We conclude that $\text{Cl}(X) = \mathbf{Z}/2\mathbf{Z}$. </p>
